{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2456 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "4f738433cde9d988f66ee01fd85de7a08a6d552a0ec91eb68945b36efd3c16f1" :edb/db-before-t 2455 :edb/db-after-t 2456 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535768 50 #inst "2026-10-09T06:50:28.227Z" 13194139535768 true] [17592186198110 1100 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, compute $P(-0.53 < Z < 0.53)$.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, 0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6628 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6664 \\,$ | $\\, 0.67 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6985 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7019 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7054 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7324 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7357 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7389 \\,$ |" 13194139535768 false] [17592186198110 1100 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z \\sim N(0,1),$ compute $P(-0.53 <   Z <        0.53).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6628 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6664 \\,$ | $\\, 0.67 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6985 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7019 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7054 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7324 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7357 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7389 \\,$ |" 13194139535768 true] [17592186198110 1145 "We're given that $Z\\sim N(0,1)$, and we wish to compute $P(-0.53 < Z < 0.53)$. The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4420/Source/Images/0b30d484a778ffede28d541415b21e58.png)\n\nBy the definition of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$,\n\n$$\nP(Z \\leq z) = \\Phi(z)\n$$\n\nSo, we express the required probability in terms of $\\Phi(z)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(-0.53 < Z < 0.53) &= P(Z < 0.53) - P(Z \\le - 0.53) \\\\\n&= P(Z \\le 0.53) - P(Z \\le - 0.53) \\\\\n&= Φ(0.53) - Φ(-0.53)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.53) = 0.7019\n$$\n\nbut we are not given $\\Phi(-0.53)$. However, we can compute this using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΦ(-0.53) &= P(Z \\le - 0.53) \\\\\n&= P(Z \\ge 0.53) \\\\\n&= 1 - P(Z < 0.53) \\\\\n&= 1 - P(Z \\le 0.53) \\\\\n&= 1 - Φ(0.53) \\\\\n&= 1 - 0.7019 \\\\\n&= 0.2981\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(-0.53 < Z < 0.53) &= Φ(0.53) - Φ(-0.53) \\\\\n&= 0.7019 - 0.2981 \\\\\n&= 0.4038\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535768 false] [17592186198110 1145 "We're given that $Z\\sim N(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P(-0.53 <        Z <        0.53).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/assets/bb86bde3595d6a64d1cd82da3c928f28c496f495f9c6815e56ee025a4a69d685.png)\n\nBy the definition of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z),$ \n\n$$\nP(Z \\leq z) = \\Phi(z).\n$$\n\nSo, we express the required probability in terms of $\\Phi(z)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.53<Z<0.53) & =P(Z<0.53)-P(Z\\le -0.53) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.53)-P(Z\\le -0.53) \\\\  & =Φ(0.53)-Φ(-0.53)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.53) = 0.7019,\n$$\n\nbut we are not given $\\Phi(-0.53) .$ However, we can compute this using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.53) & =P(Z\\le -0.53) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 0.53) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<0.53) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.53) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.53) \\\\  & =1-0.7019 \\\\  & =0.2981\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.53<Z<0.53) & =Φ(0.53)-Φ(-0.53) \\\\  & =0.7019-0.2981 \\\\  & =0.4038.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535768 true] [17592186198111 1100 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, compute $P(Z \\gt -0.76)$.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ | $\\, 0.06 \\,$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, 0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7422 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7454 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7486 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7734 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7764 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7794 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.8 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8023 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8051 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8078 \\,$ |" 13194139535768 false] [17592186198111 1100 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z \\sim N(0,1),$ compute $P(Z \\gt -0.76).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ | $\\, 0.06 \\,$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7422 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7454 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7486 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7734 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7764 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7794 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.8 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8023 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8051 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8078 \\,$ |" 13194139535768 true] [17592186198111 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/assets/f88bf9a66694858e9eaf16f616555eda6d9da7de9f62b678bc1f03b32bf9a620.png)\n\nFor a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1)$ we have the following symmetry property for $a > 0{:}$\n\n$$\nP(Z \\gt -a) = P(Z \\lt a) = \\Phi(a)\n$$\n\nWe're given that $Z\\sim N(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P( Z > -0.76).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/assets/fbacbbe3f53c73316fd070d95e4c077cd695bdcf884d30a95f6b0850df328c80.png)\n\nBy symmetry, this equals the area corresponding to the probability $P(Z \\lt 0.76).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/assets/c0f612336c02eee97d6c66b56f0854286b3a23b90053810e9e4fb75b698b5a60.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Z > -0.76)\n= P(Z < 0.76)\n= \\Phi(0.76)\n= 0.7764.\n$$" 13194139535768 true] [17592186198111 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4420/Source/Images/66fefa957dd168d1589cdb34bce3bced.png)\n\nFor a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1)$ we have the following symmetry property for $a > 0{:}$\n\n$$\nP(Z \\gt -a) = P(Z \\lt a) = \\Phi(a)\n$$\n\nWe're given that $Z\\sim N(0,1)$, and we wish to compute $P(Z > -0.76)$. The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4420/Source/Images/1bc0f3dd71234ca7671872a937519a38.png)\n\nBy symmetry, this equals the area corresponding to the probability $P(Z \\lt 0.76)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4420/Source/Images/bd8ed379c32619927570445941878c4f.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Z > -0.76) = P(Z < 0.76) = \\Phi(0.76) = 0.7764\n$$" 13194139535768 false] [17592186198112 1100 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, compute $P(Z \\lt -1.54)$.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, 1.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9066 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9082 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9099 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9222 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9236 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9251 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9357 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9370 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9382 \\,$ |" 13194139535768 false] [17592186198112 1100 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z \\sim N(0,1),$ compute $P(Z \\lt -1.54).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 1.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9066 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9082 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9099 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9222 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9236 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9251 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9357 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9370 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9382 \\,$ |" 13194139535768 true] [17592186198112 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/assets/4d6524ff1e32b2c2b4aaf7481dbf63a96f847cd91ce66aeeb58690f37bbe50e5.png)\n\nFor a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1),$ we have the following symmetry property for $a > 0{:}$\n\n$$\nP(Z \\gt a) = P(Z \\lt -a) = \\Phi(-a)\n$$\n\nWe're given that $Z\\sim N(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P( Z \\lt -1.54).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/assets/52d4c1add91819ff1f9dddf609b5753506c26e6cd6ee7f3cb14150ccb9b60971.png)\n\nBy symmetry, this equals the area corresponding to the probability $P(Z \\gt 1.54).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/assets/7fef056bb5564986a009198685a2951de87ccb8e09c932c6902bfafdcdf0656f.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z<-1.54) & =P(Z>1.54) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<1.54) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.54) \\\\  & =1-0.9382 \\\\  & =0.0618.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535768 true] [17592186198112 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4420/Source/Images/95c129d15152dcce16214fa0613e8fb6.png)\n\nFor a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, we have the following symmetry property for $a > 0{:}$\n\n$$\nP(Z \\gt a) = P(Z \\lt -a) = \\Phi(-a)\n$$\n\nWe're given that $Z\\sim N(0,1)$, and we wish to compute $P(Z \\lt -1.54)$. The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4420/Source/Images/aa5d3354086f3622c4f7c5920b1753b8.png)\n\nBy symmetry, this equals the area corresponding to the probability $P(Z \\gt 1.54)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4420/Source/Images/3e0d56a379a7fecf3403b9750a7133ab.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Z < - 1.54) &= P(Z > 1.54) \\\\\n&= 1 - P(Z < 1.54) \\\\\n&= 1 - Φ(1.54) \\\\\n&= 1 - 0.9382 \\\\\n&= 0.0618\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535768 false] [17592186198394 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365304 13194139535768 true] [17592186198394 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365311 13194139535768 true] [17592186198394 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365339 13194139535768 true] [17592186198394 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365342 13194139535768 true] [17592186198394 1020 #